Q: Unattended (no-prompt) Homebrew installation using expect A: <pre><code>NONINTERACTIVE=1 /bin/bash -c &quot;$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Homebrew/install/HEAD/install.sh)&quot;
</code></pre>
<p>In 2026 You can do this.</p>

Q: Is it okay to use setState inside a setState callback? A: <p>Nested state setters are technically possible but not officially recommended.</p>
<p>The problem with code is that it tries to keep <code>newThing</code> reference stable by not using <code>things</code> state dependency as <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79893182/3731501">this answer suggests</a> but still uses <code>setThings</code> and <code>setSelectedThingId</code> as dependencies, through state setter functions are expected to be stable with <code>useState</code>. And <code>deleteThing</code> uses <code>selectedThingId</code> dependency, so nested state setters don't solve the whole problem. As a result, callbacks are still unstable, and depending on how they are used, re-registering them can be inconvenient.</p>
<p>A potentially cleaner and more maintainable way is to use effects. <code>things</code> and <code>selectedThingId</code> are linked states, this emphasizes their relation. Considering state setters are stable, this would require no unnecessary dependencies, but this requires to keep current state value in a ref to access it in a callback. The actual state synchronization logic depends on the details of the case:</p>
<pre><code>const selectedThingIdRef = useRef();
selectedThingIdRef.current = selectedThingId;

const newThing = useCallback(() =&gt; {
  setThings(things =&gt; {
    const newId = 1 + Math.max(...things.map(thing =&gt; thing.id));
    return [...things, { id: newId, name: 'New Thing' }];
  });
}, []);

const deleteThing = useCallback(() =&gt; {
  setThings(things =&gt; things.filter(thing =&gt; thing.id !== selectedThingIdRef.current));
}, []);

useEffect(() =&gt; {
  setSelectedThingId(id =&gt; {
    const exists = things.some(thing =&gt; thing.id === id);
    return exists ? id : (things[0]?.id ?? 0);
  });
}, [things]);
</code></pre>
<p>The use of a state setter inside an effect can trigger linter error related to <a href="https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect#updating-state-based-on-props-or-state" rel="nofollow noreferrer">effect-related</a> rules of hooks, which are opinionated but conventionally followed. In this case this would be a false positive. Specifically for state setter, <a href="https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect#adjusting-some-state-when-a-prop-changes" rel="nofollow noreferrer">it's allowed to be used outside an effect as long as it's conditional and doesn't cause recursive updates</a>, but this would require additionally tracking the changes  in <code>things</code> to prevent this, something that <code>useEffect</code> naturally does.</p>
<p>A simpler alternative to <code>selectedThingIdRef</code> workaround would be <a href="https://github.com/reactjs/rfcs/blob/useevent/text/0000-useevent.md" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>useEvent</code> proposal that was intended for callbacks</a>, it provided stable function reference while giving access to component scope. This resulted in <code>useEffectEvent</code> that has similar implementation but doesn't provide a stable reference and the usage is limited to effects. The original <code>useEvent</code> implementation is available via <a href="https://github.com/scottrippey/react-use-event-hook" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>react-use-event-hook</code> package</a>:</p>
<pre><code>const newThing = useEvent(() =&gt; {
  const newId = 1 + Math.max(...things.map(thing =&gt; thing.id));

  setSelectedThingId(newId);

  setThings([
    ...things,
    { id: newId, name: 'New Thing' },
  ]);
});
</code></pre>
<p>Same for <code>deleteThing</code>, no dependency array is needed for stable reference.</p>

Q: Angular error using new input signal transform to update other property in component A: <p>It's an anti-pattern, input transforms should not be used to sync changes of an input with other parts of the application. You should use an <a href="https://angular.dev/guide/signals#effects" rel="nofollow noreferrer">effect</a> instead:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>updatedShift = input&lt;ShiftUpsert, ShiftUpsert&gt;({ shiftId: 0 });
constructor() {
  effect(() =&gt; this.shiftForm.patchValue(value));
}
</code></pre>
